# 1. Project Overview

## 1.1 Objective

Build an automated Android code auditing system based on multiple specialized agents.

Instead of relying on a single agent to analyze the entire codebase, the system distributes the auditing process among independent agents, each responsible for a specific technical domain.

A central agent, called `android-audit-orchestrator`, coordinates the execution of all specialized agents and consolidates their results.

Each agent must have:

- Clearly defined responsibilities.
- Dedicated technical skills.
- Independent auditing rules.
- Access to the Android source code.
- The ability to identify code violations.
- Independent reporting capabilities.
- Structured and verifiable findings.

The system must prioritize real defects, minimize false positives, and provide actionable recommendations.

## 1.2 Main Goals

1. Analyze complete Android projects.
2. Analyze only modified files in Pull Requests or Gerrit changes.
3. Execute independent audits by technical specialization.
4. Support parallel agent execution when possible.
5. Identify real problems and avoid speculative findings.
6. Detect duplicate findings across agents.
7. Generate individual reports for every agent.
8. Generate a consolidated executive report.
9. Provide concrete recommendations for fixing identified problems.
10. Support future Gerrit and CI/CD integration.

