# 5. Orchestrator Responsibilities

The `android-audit-orchestrator` is the central coordinator.

It must not replace specialized agents or perform all audits itself.

Its primary responsibility is to distribute work, validate results, and produce consolidated reports.

## 5.1 Main responsibilities

1. Discover the Android project.
2. Identify project modules.
3. Detect Kotlin, Java, XML, and Compose files.
4. Identify the requested audit scope.
5. Discover available agents and skills.
6. Determine which agents are relevant.
7. Dispatch audit tasks.
8. Execute independent agents in parallel when supported.
9. Collect agent results.
10. Validate findings.
11. Detect duplicate findings.
12. Prioritize issues.
13. Generate individual reports.
14. Generate the executive report.
15. Preserve traceability between findings and source files.

## 5.2 Orchestrator workflow

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Start Audit"] --> B["Discover Android Project"]
    B --> C["Analyze Project Structure"]
    C --> D["Determine Audit Scope"]
    D --> E["Discover Agents and Skills"]
    E --> F["Build Execution Plan"]
    F --> G["Dispatch Specialized Agents"]
    G --> H["Collect Agent Results"]
    H --> I["Validate Findings"]
    I --> J["Deduplicate Findings"]
    J --> K["Prioritize by Severity"]
    K --> L["Generate Individual Reports"]
    L --> M["Generate Executive Report"]
    M --> N["Audit Completed"]
```

## 5.3 Agent execution rules

- Execute independent agents in parallel when the execution environment supports it.
- Do not launch duplicate audits for the same agent and scope.
- Pass a clearly defined task to each agent.
- Provide project root and audit scope.
- Require structured output.
- Do not let agents modify source code.
- Wait for all required agents before generating the final consolidated report.
- Record agents that failed, timed out, or were skipped.
- Do not report an incomplete audit as successful.

If parallel execution is unavailable, agents may run sequentially without changing their responsibilities.

