# 6. Audit Execution Modes

The system must support multiple execution modes.

## 6.1 Full Audit

Command:

```bash
android-audit --full
```

Purpose:

Analyze the complete Android project.

Expected behavior:

- Discover all relevant modules.
- Analyze all supported source files.
- Execute all eight agents.
- Generate individual reports.
- Generate consolidated report.

## 6.2 Changed Files Audit

Command:

```bash
android-audit --changed-only
```

Purpose:

Analyze only files modified in the current Git change.

Expected behavior:

- Identify the base Git revision.
- Identify modified, added, and renamed files.
- Identify deleted files for contextual analysis.
- Map files to relevant agents.
- Analyze changed code and necessary surrounding context.
- Report only actionable findings introduced or directly exposed by the change.

Important:

A finding in a modified file is not automatically a new regression. The agent must inspect the diff and determine whether the problem was introduced by the change.

## 6.3 Single Agent Audit

Command:

```bash
android-audit --agent compose
```

Supported agents:

```text
architecture
kotlin
compose
performance
memory
testing
security
build
```

Purpose:

Execute only the selected agent.

## 6.4 Module Audit

Command:

```bash
android-audit --module feature.store
```

Purpose:

Audit a specific Gradle module and relevant dependencies.

## 6.5 Custom Audit

Command:

```bash
android-audit --scope changed --agents compose,kotlin,architecture
```

Purpose:

Allow users to select the audit scope and agents.

