# 11. Claude Code Implementation

The system must use Claude Code's native agent and skill mechanisms.

## 11.1 Agent definitions

Each specialized agent must be defined in:

```text
.claude/agents/
```

Use the Claude Code subagent definition format, including:

- Agent name.
- Description.
- Supported tools.
- Model configuration when necessary.
- System instructions.
- Skills to use.
- Required output format.

Agent definitions must explicitly identify which skills they are responsible for applying.

## 11.2 Skill definitions

Each skill must be located in:

```text
.claude/skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md
```

Skills provide reusable technical auditing instructions.

An agent must load and apply its assigned skills before analyzing code.

Do not copy all skill rules into agent definitions. Keep specialized technical knowledge in the skills to avoid duplicated and inconsistent instructions.

## 11.3 Orchestrator definition

The orchestrator must be defined as a Claude Code subagent or equivalent coordinating entry point.

Its responsibilities include:

- Discovering the available agents.
- Preparing audit tasks.
- Invoking specialized subagents through the supported Claude Code mechanism.
- Collecting their responses.
- Validating structured output.
- Generating reports.

The orchestration implementation must use actual supported Claude Code subagent invocation mechanisms. Do not assume that merely writing an `AGENT.md` or `SKILL.md` automatically executes another agent.

